    <briefdescription>
<para>C++20 concept satisfied when <computeroutput>T</computeroutput> provides both <computeroutput>begin()</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>end()</computeroutput> member functions returning an iterator and its sentinel. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para><parameterlist kind="templateparam"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>T</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The type to be checked.</para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
The <computeroutput>container_like</computeroutput> concept is satisfied when <computeroutput>T</computeroutput> exposes both a <computeroutput>begin()</computeroutput> and an <computeroutput>end()</computeroutput> member function, as required by standard range-based iteration, and <computeroutput>begin()</computeroutput> returns a <computeroutput>std::input_or_output_iterator</computeroutput> for which <computeroutput>end()</computeroutput> returns a <computeroutput>std::sentinel_for</computeroutput>. The check is part of the concept, so a type whose <computeroutput>begin()</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>end()</computeroutput> return something else (<computeroutput>void</computeroutput>, for example) is not a container, instead of being a hard error where the element type is formed. It is used to detect the ranges that the formatter writes element by element (see <computeroutput>formattable_range</computeroutput>), and the ranges of values of <computeroutput>test_each()</computeroutput>. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
